WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.760
Let’s talk about the war in Syria.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.240
Entire cities flattened ... 

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.240
hospitals bombed … 

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.520
ancient sites blown up … 

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.160
13 million people forced to leave their homes.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.920
At one point the UN said the battlefield chaos made it impossible to count the dead. So they stopped.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.760
But the fighting hasn’t and people are still dying.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.760
So who’s fighting who? 

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.400
Where are Syria’s millions of refugees? 

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.520
And why does this seem like a war without end?

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.840
For years the Syrian war was the biggest news story around. Today, not so much. 

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:18.520
Maybe it’s media fatigue, maybe people think it’s over and that President Bashar al-Assad has won.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.600
The Syrian government seems to think so.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.840
Cities and railways are being rebuilt.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.160
Domestic flights have started up again. 

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.560
They’re even letting travel vloggers in.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.240
In reality, though, most of Syria is still in ruins.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.280
It all started back in 2011.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.400
The Arab Spring was happening and leaders in Tunisia and Egypt had already been overthrown.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.120
In Syria some children had been watching all that on TV and decided to send their president a message.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.080
The children were arrested and tortured and people started protesting.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.480
The government responded with force and the Syrian uprising was on.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.480
The Assad regime had been in power for more than 40 years — starting with Bashar’s father.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.080
Many people were tired of things like corruption, being repressed. They wanted al-Assad out.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:19.440
When the war started it was very much a civil war — Assad forces against the Syrian opposition. 

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.280
That included people who defected from the military and opponents who picked up a gun. 

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.680
Then other players piled in.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.720
The US, Turkey and others sided with the opposition pretty much from the start.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.200
The Syrian president had allies too.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.000
Russian warplanes bombed from the air and militias backed by Iran fought on the ground.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.720
But there were other enemies common to them all.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.680
ISIL, al-Nusra Front and other groups considered terrorists took advantage of the chaos to take control.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.520
Then Syrian Kurdish fighters got involved. 

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:58.360
They joined up with Arab militias to push ISIL out and stake their own territorial claim.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:05.320
Turkey, though, sees them as terrorists and didn’t want them on its southern border so they went to war with the Syrian Kurds.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.640
It’s been a messy battlefield. 

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.160
And over the course of those 10 years the Syrian people have suffered the most.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.440
People who weren’t part of that war were barrel bombed by the Assad regime … 

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.600
attacked with chemical weapons … 

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.080
blockaded, starved and detained — mainly by Assad forces.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.520
Victims have filed allegations of war crimes in courts all over Europe.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.560
Things were bad during the peak of the war. 

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.760
But right now the economy has never been worse.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.720
Nearly 90% of Syrians live below the poverty line.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:05.080
Their currency, the pound, is at its lowest ever level against the dollar, making everything expensive.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:17.400
There are shortages of fuel, food — some places only have electricity for a few hours a day.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.480
Add to that a COVID-19 epidemic that no one knows the real scale of.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.320
OK, so where is the war at right now?

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.680
These days the government has control of about two-thirds of the country.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.840
But they’re still fighting for parts of the north where all kinds of groups are still hanging on.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.240
Some Syrian rebels are in one part where Turkey’s basically in control.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.840
And then there’s Idlib — the last opposition stronghold. 

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.880
It’s a complicated place. Syrian rebels are mixed in with lots of groups considered terrorists.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.520
Millions of refugees are living there in camps.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.760
And every so often Syrian and Russian forces attack.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.120
They say they’re liberating Idlib from terrorists.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.920
Meanwhile Syrian Kurds control this region. 

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.480
Hundreds of US soldiers are still there, too, mainly protecting oil fields.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.320
But there are other groups roaming around, including militias backed by Iran like Hezbollah.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.240
You’ll notice, though, there’s no sign of ISIL anywhere.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.480
It took a while but eventually they were pushed out.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:07.240
Their leader, Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi, was killed by the US in 2019 and most of their fighters were either captured or killed.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:13.480
 But ISIL’s ideology has survived and it’s spreading in camps where fighters and their families are being held.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:28.320
So that’s where the conflict is at. 

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.800
But you can’t talk about a nation without talking about its people — and half of the Syrian people are refugees.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:41.240
More than 6 million are internally displaced.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.960
But another 5.5 million are living outside of Syria. 

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.040
Turkey’s taken in the most followed by Lebanon, Jordan, Iraq and Egypt.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.480
Over the years there have been countless peace talks and all of them have failed.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:08:00.840
Right now the UN has put together a committee made up of both opposition and government people.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.000
It’s to try to turn the page on the war by writing a new constitution.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.120
But it’s not really working. 

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.440
Meanwhile there are all those external players still using Syria to settle their scores.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:28.080
There’s an argument to be made that this war really is over, that the Assad regime has won.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:35.760
If that’s true it’s come at an enormous cost, and 10 years on, things in Syria have never been worse.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:42.240
Ten years of war in less than 10 minutes is a big ask.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.520
So think of that episode as a primer. 

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:49.400
You can visit aljazeera.com for more context and the latest news out of Syria. 

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:50.960
 I’ll see you next week.

